## ABSTRACT
Development of ease-fabricated and effectively self-disinfecting textile materials for antimicrobial and infection prevention has been urgently desired by both consumers and industry. However, some nonresponsive antibacterial agents finished fabrics may be harmful to human. To address this issue, we developed a facile finishing method to endow woven cotton fabrics (WCF) with light-driven antibacterial property. Here in, porphyrinic metal-organic frameworks (PCN-224) were in situ synthesized on WCF (termed PCN-224/WCF) and PCN-224/WCF was proven to be used for antibacterial photodynamic inactivation (aPDI). aPDI studies indicated no difference in bacterial inactivation, the inactivation was $99.9999\%$ of Gram-negative Escherichia coli 8099 and Pseudomonas aeruginosa CMCC (B) 10104 as well as Gram-positive Staphylococcus aureus ATCC-6538 and Bacillus subtilis CMCC (B) 63501 under visible light illumination (500 W, 15 cm vertical distance, $\lambda{\geq}420\ \mathrm{nm}$ , 45 min). Cytotoxicity tests revealed PCN-224/WCF had low biological toxicity and good biocompatibility. Mechanism study revealed that singlet oxygen $({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2})$ was produced by PCN-224/WCF and caused severe damage to bacteria which was observed from the SEM images. This study provided a facile guideline to functionalize cotton fabrics with responsive bactericidal property which showed great potential for new generation of textiles with practical applications.
KEYWORD: cotton fabric, self-disinfecting, metal-organic frameworks, antibacterial photodynamic inactivation
## 1. INTRODUCTION
Cotton fiber is one of the most widely used natural fibers for textile materials due to its breathability, softness, degradability and ease of mass production [1, 2]. In practice, cotton textile materials could be endowed with multiple functions, including self-cleaning super hydrophobic [3-5], electrical conductivity [6, 7], electromagnetic interference shielding [8], flame retardation [9], and antibacterial functionalization [10]. Cotton textiles are widely used in clothing because of their sweat absorption, quick drying, warmth retention, and physiological comfortable to wear. However, the porous structure and sweat absorption properties of cotton fabrics provide perfect space and nutrition for the growth and survival of pathogens that seriously threaten human being's life and health [11]. Besides, cotton clothes are susceptible to mould and bacteria during storage. Especially for healthcare workers and patients, cotton medical textiles emphasize the need for self-disinfecting materials to prevent the transmission of pathogens [12]. Therefore, some strategies need to be adopted to reduce the possibility of microbial infection and transmission, so that further applications of cotton textiles would not be hindered.
To address these issues, self-disinfecting cotton fabrics have attracted a lot of attention [13, 14]. Antibacterial cellulose fabrics could inhibit the growth of bacteria and microorganisms, or even kill them [15]. For this purpose, some efforts have been dedicated to combine cotton fabrics with a variety of antimicrobial agents, such as nanoparticles [16-19], organic quaternary ammonium salts [20, 21], oxide particles [22- 26], metal salts [27, 28], silver [29], antibiotics [30], and some specific molecules [31-
33]. Although these modified fabrics presented good antimicrobial, the shortcomings could not be ignored, such as narrow spectrum, toxicity to the skin, and resistance. Faced with the above issues, novel modification approaches are urgently needed for functionalizing cotton fabrics with antibacterial property.
One potential approach for combining cotton fabrics with antibacterial property is to introduce aPDI, which is a versatile and promising technology for reducing the transmission of infectious diseases [34]. aPDI presents no toxicity in the absence of light and causes no resistance [35-37]. There are three key components in need for aPDI: nontoxic photosensitizer (PS), non-injurious light illumination (usually visible light) and molecular oxygen. Reactive oxygen species (ROS) could be produced by photochemical reaction of the above three components from two mechanisms (Type I and/or Type II mechanisms) [38, 39], including hydrogen peroxide, hydroxyl radicals and superoxide anion radical (Type I) and ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ (Type II). In general, aPDI has the following two prominent advantages: (i) as an novel strategy to treat bacterial infections, aPDI is effective against both drug-sensitive and drug-resistant pathogens [40]; (ii) since ROS produces non-specific damage, aPDI does not develop resistance [41]. Under the threat of bacteria, the above advantages make aPDI have great application prospects.
One key component of aPDI is PS. Currently available PSs, such as synthetic and natural tetrapyrroles [42, 43], phthalocyanines [44], phenothiazines (methylene blue) [45, 46], and xanthene dyes (rose Bengal) [47, 48] have been successfully utilized in aPDI. Although these PSs could be used as effective photo-medicine, they may have one or more shortcomings that would limit their further applications, such as poor water solubility, undesirable photophysical properties, complex synthetic routes, and/or refractory to biodegradation/biocompatibility. To overcome part of the drawbacks, a lot of efforts have been made including synthesis process optimization [49, 50], coupling water-soluble target reagent [51-53], and the use of a carrier or functional encapsulation [54-57].
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), which are porous crystalline nanoscale materials, are emerging as a kind of novel polymer with metal ions and organic ligands via coordination bonds [58-60]. Because of the simple synthesis steps, intrinsic porosity, and easy controllability, MOFs have exhibited ever-expanding applications in catalysis [61-63], gas storage [58], chemical sensing [64], drug delivery [65], and energy [66]. Most recently, porphyrin introduced MOFs have been reported to generate ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ with high efficiency and kill target cancer cells [67, 68]. Among all the porphyrin introduced MOFs, PCN-224 (PCN: Porous Coordination Network) has emerged as a class of potential PS with many favorable advantages for aPDI [69], including ease-of-synthesis, high-efficiency, nontoxicity, remarkably stable to acids and bases, and the special porous structure is helpful for the diffusion of ROS [70]. Given these recent advantages, some questions have arisen about the scope and applications of PCN-224 as PS. For instance, since PCN-224 can produce ROS, can it be used as a new antibacterial agent against both Gram-negative and Gram-positive bacteria? If so, can PCN-224 be introduced to cotton fabric to be made into self-disinfecting material?
To solve these problems and inspired by previous studies [71-73], we reported the fabrication of MOFs decorated WCF as a high efficient flexible textile material for aPDI. PCN-224 was synthesized on the surface of cotton fiber by in situ growth which could provide much more convenience than other finishing method. aPDI studies revealed no bacterial inactivation difference towards Gram-negative and Gram-positive bacterial strains. 3-(4,5-dimethylthiazol-2-yl)-2, 5-diphenyltetrazolium bromide (MTT) assay with L929 cells was performed to evaluate the good biocompatible property of the materials. Mechanism study revealed ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ was produced by PCN-224/WCF under illumination. Taken together, we provided a facile strategy to endow cotton fabrics with light-driven broad spectrum antibacterial property and the materials presented low cytotoxicity, which showed great potential for the practical applications of health care materials.
## 2. EXPERIMENTAL
## 2.1 Materials and reagents
Woven cotton fabrics (WCF, thickness: $292.67{\scriptstyle\pm5.69}$ μm, $170\mathrm{~g~m}^{-2})$ were   
purchased from Wan Chang textile Co., Ltd (Jinzhou, China). 1,3-   
diphenylisobenzofuran (DPBF, $98\%$ ), 5,10,15,20-Tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl)porphyrin   
(TCPP, $97\%$ ), and $Z_{\mathrm{{rOCl}_{2}\cdot8H_{2}O}}$ $(99\%)$ were obtained by Shanghai Vita Chemical   
Reagent Co., Ltd. Benzoic acid (BA, $99.5\%$ ), dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO, $99.9\%$ ),   
ethanol $(99.7\%)$ ), methanol $(99.5\%)$ , tween-80 $(99.5\%)$ , potassium chloride (KCl,   
$99.5\%$ ), sodium chloride (NaCl, $99.8\%$ ), N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF, $99\%$ ), plate   
count agar, glutaric dialdehyde $(25-28\%)$ ), disodium hydrogen phosphate $\mathrm{(Na_{2}H P O_{4}}$ ,   
$99\%$ ), monopotassium phosphate $\mathrm{\KH_{2}P O_{4}}$ , $99.5\%$ , sodium carbonate $\mathrm{(Na_{2}C O_{3}}$ , 6
$99.8\%$ ), yeast extract $(99.5\%)$ , tryptone and soy peptone were obtained from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. All the reagents were obtained without purification and deionized water was sterilized before all the experiments.
## 2.2 Fabrication of PCN-224 and PCN-224/WCF
For the synthesis of PCN-224 nanoparticles, 0.13 mmol TCPP ( $\mathrm{100mg)}$ , 23 mmol BA $(2.8\mathrm{g})$ and 0.93 mmol $\mathrm{ZrOCl}_{2}$ · $8\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ( $300\mathrm{mg},$ ) were added into 100 mL DMF. Then, the mixture was under sonication for $10\mathrm{min}$ , followed by heated by $90^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $5\mathrm{h}$ under mechanical stirring. When the temperature of the reaction system was cooled down to room temperature naturally, PCN-224 particles were separated by centrifugation $(11525\times\mathrm{g},30\mathrm{min})$ . The separated particles were washed with fresh DMF and ethanol for five times. Finally, the particles were freeze-dried and stored away from light. The PCN-224/WCF was prepared using a similar procedure. Briefly, a piece of WCF (100 $\times100\mathrm{mm}\mathrm{,}$ ) was immersed in the mixture solution for 10 min under sonication, followed by heated by $90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $5\mathrm{h}$ under mechanical stirring. When the reaction finished and the temperature was cooled down, the substrate was taken out and washed for five times with fresh DMF and ethanol. Finally, the PCN-224/WCF was freeze-dried for characterization and aPDI study.
## 2.3 Characterization
The fiber surface morphology before and after PCN-224 growth was observed by SEM (SU1510, Hitachi). The size distribution and morphology of PCN-224 nanoparticles were determined by TEM (JEM-2100). The wettability of the fabrics was tested by a contact angle testing system (DCAT-21). Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FT-IR) was recorded by a Nicolet Nexus spectrometer (USA). X-ray diffraction analysis spectrum (XRD) was obtained by X-ray diffractometer (Rigaku Ultimate IV). Elemental analysis (EDS) was obtained by EDAX Octane Elect Energy Dispersive Spectroscopy instrument. Absorbance spectra of WCF and PCN-224/WCF were recorded on a Shimadzu spectrophotometer (UV-3600) with a wavelength range of $400{-}800\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The mechanical property was obtained by a fabric strength tester (YG028), the prepared fabric samples were $5\times100\mathrm{mm}$ . The clamping distance was 50 mm and the tensile speed was $20\ \mathrm{\mm/min}$ . Thermogravimetric and derivative thermogravimetry analysis (TGA and DTG) was obtained by TGA 5500 (USA), heating rate was set $10\ \mathrm{{^circC/min}}$ with nitrogen flowing.
## 2.4 Culturing bacteria
Escherichia coli, Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Staphylococcus aureus, and Bacillus subtilis were selected as typical bacterial strains to explore the universality of antibacterial of the PCN-224/WCF material. Briefly, lysogeny broth (LB) was used as the media to culture Escherichia coli ATCC 8099 at $37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10{-}12\mathrm{~h~}$ in an orbital incubator $(120\ \mathrm{rpm})$ . Staphylococcus aureus ATCC 6538, Pseudomonas aeruginosa CMCC(B) 10104 and Bacillus subtilis CMCC(B) 63501 were cultured by the same procedure, but changing LB to tryptic soy broth (TSB). The initial concentration of bacterial strains was about $10^{8}$ colony forming units $\mathrm{(CFU)/mL}$ . Cultures were separated by centrifugation $(6820\times\mathrm{g},10\mathrm{min})$ , and then the cultures were resuspended into PBS solution. (PBS aqueous solution: 170 mM NaCl, 10 mM ${\mathrm{Na}}_{2}{\mathrm{HPO}}_{4}$ , $1.8~\mathrm{mM}$ ${\mathrm{KH}}_{2}{\mathrm{PO}}_{4}.$ , $3.4\mathrm{mM}$ KCl, $0.05\%$ Tween-80, $\mathrm{pH}{=}7.4$ ). PBS and broth were sterilized prior
to experiments.
## 2.5 Antibacterial photodynamic inactivation instrumentation
aPDI study of the PCN-224/WCF material was performed with a 500 W Xenon arc lamp. The vertical distance of illumination is $15\ \mathrm{cm}$ with an optical filter $\lambda{\ge}420$ nm).
## 2.6 Antibacterial photodynamic inactivation assay
aPDI against four bacterial stains were performed in 24-well plate. Typically, PCN-224/WCF $10\times10~\mathrm{mm}$ was first put on the bottom of three adjacent wells in a 24-well plate, then $0.1\mathrm{mL}$ PBS solution containing bacteria was dripped on the surface of PCN-224/WCF. The plate was kept away from light for $60\mathrm{min}$ and then illuminated (Xe lamp, 500 W, $\lambda{\ge}420$ nm, 45 min, 15 cm). All the antibacterial inactivation experiments were performed in triplicate, with dark control, WCF light control and fabric-free dark control experiments.
When the illumination was done, $0.9\mathrm{mL}$ absolute PBS solution was added to the illuminated and control 24-well plate. Then $0.1\mathrm{mL}$ of the above solution was taken out and diluted by ten times. The 1:10 dilution was conducted by six times. Then each series was plated on the agar culture plate (LB-agar culture plate: E. coli, TSB-agar culture plate: S. aureus, P. aeruginosa and B. subtilis). The plates were placed in dark at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for about $15{-}24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The highest detectable antibacterial inactivation data was limited because of the plating method. With the initial concentration of $10^{8}\mathrm{CFU/mL}$ , 6 log unit detection in CFU/mL were possible. Survivability values which were limited to $20.0001\%$ represented the detection limit. Statistical significance was evaluated with an unpaired Student’s two-tailed t-test [74, 75].
## 2.7 Morphological observation of bacteria
SEM was used to observe the morphological change of bacteria. Briefly, after illumination, PCN-224/WCF was soaked in glutaraldehyde $(2.5\%)$ at $2{-}8~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight in order to make the morphology of bacteria fixed. Then, the bacteria attached to the fibers was dehydrated (ethanol: $25\%$ , $50\%$ , $75\%$ , $95\%$ , and $100\%$ ). Finally, the bacteria were freeze-dried before visualization $(15000\times)$ .
## 2.8 Cell viability assay
The cytotoxicity of PCN-224/WCF was evaluated by MTT assay. L929 cells were cultured by Dulbecco’s Modified Eagle’s Medium (DMEM, Gibco, USA), with 100 $\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ penicillin, $10\%$ fetal bovine serum (FBS) and $100~\mathrm{\upmug/mL}$ streptomycin. Briefly, PCN-224/WCF was sterilized first and then L929 cells were seeded with PCN224/WCF. After $24\mathrm{~h~}$ of incubation, the medium was removed and $0.1~\mathrm{mL}$ MTT solution $\mathrm{0.5\:mg/mL}$ ) was added. The 96-well plate was incubated at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $4\mathrm{h}$ in a humid environment with $5\%$ carbon dioxide, then $0.1~\mathrm{mL}$ of the above solution from each well was replaced by DMSO. After gently shaking for 10 min, $0.1~\mathrm{mL}$ solution was transferred to an another 96-well plate. Absorbance at $570\ \mathrm{nm}$ was recorded with microplate spectrophotometer (EPOCH2T).
## 2.9 Detection of ROS $(^{1}\mathbf{O}_{2})$
DPBF, which could be used as a quencher to detect ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ , was selected to identify the ROS produced by PCN-224/WCF. Briefly, a piece of PCN-224/WCF or WCF (10 $\times\ 10\ \mathrm{mm}$ ) was placed on the bottom of the beaker, then absolute methanol containing
$74\upmu\mathrm{M}$ DPBF was added. The mixing system was preserved in dark at room temperature until the equilibrium absorption of DPBF on PCN-224. After that, methanol containing DPBF in the presence of fabrics was illuminated by a $532~\mathrm{nm}$ laser $(85{\pm}1~\mathrm{mW/cm^{2}}$ ), and the UV-visible spectrum was recorded every 2 min.
## 3. RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
## 3.1 Characterization
Here in, we designed and prepared high efficient light-driven photodynamic antibacterial textile materials based on PCN-224 nanoparticles by in situ growth method. WCF $\mathrm{17\mg/cm^{2}}$ were selected as the flexible support for photosensitive PCN-224, PCN-224 was successfully in situ grown on cotton fibers surface. Figure 1 presents the fabrication of PCN-224/WCF $18.55~\mathrm{mg/cm^{2}})$ and its photodynamic antibacterial property against bacteria. From the change in weight, the increased proportion of PCN224 attached on the cotton fabrics is $9.12\%$ . The SEM images of $E$ . coli in Figure 1 presents intact and smooth surfaces before illumination, however, the bacteria exhibited significant cellular structure damage after illumination, we ascribed this cellular damage to ROS (predominantly ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ , see part 3.4).
PCN-224 nanoparticles were successfully characterized by TEM and XRD. As shown in Figure S1, PCN-224 nanoparticles were well dispersed and shaped spherical. The particle size of PCN-224 mostly distributed from 57 to $120~\mathrm{nm}$ and the average size was around $88\mathrm{nm}$ . Figure S2 presents the XRD pattern of PCN-224, the different peaks at $2\theta{=}4.62^{\circ},6.54^{\circ},7.90^{\circ},9.12^{\circ},$ and $11.14^{\circ}$ represented the crystal plane of [002], [022], [222], [004], and [224], respectively [76, 77]. The characterization results reveals that PCN-224 nanoparticles had been successfully synthesized.
Figure 2 panel A and B shows the photographic images, SEM images (50 and $3000~\times)$ , and contact angles of WCF and PCN-224/WCF. The only difference from the macroscopical photographic images was color. WCF looked white while PCN
224/WCF appeared brown-red. The colored PCN-224/WCF may simplify or omit the subsequent dyeing process [78]. From the images after $50\times$ magnification by SEM, the yarn interlacing structure could be seen clearly for both fabrics, cotton yarns were woven to fabrics with each filling yarn passed one warp yarn and under the adjacent warp. When magnification was $3000\times,$ the difference in the morphology of cotton fibers before and after the deposition of PCN-224 could be obviously observed, the unmodified cotton fibers showed the smooth surface while PCN-224 nanoparticles aggregated on the cotton fiber surface of PCN-224/WCF after PCN-224 loading. The growth mechanism of PCN-224 nanoparticles on cotton fibers may be attributed to two aspects, electrostatic interaction and hydrogen bonding [15, 79]. Metal ions were first absorbed on the surface of cotton fiber by electrostatic interaction, then the other substances reacted with metal ions to form PCN-224 on the surface of the fiber [80]; rich hydroxyl groups of the cotton fiber may interact with metal ions or the metal-OH by hydrogen bonding [81, 82]. The wettability of the fabrics was determined by a contact angle measurement (inserts in Figure 2 panel A and B). As expected, the contact angle (PBS solution) was $0~^{\circ}$ for both WCF and PCN-224/WCF, indicating PCN-224 nanoparticles growth did not affect the hydrophilicity. However, the excellent wettability of photobactericidal materials played an important role in aPDI [83].
FT-IR was employed to identify the functional groups of WCF, PCN-224/WCF, and PCN-224 (Figure 2 panel C). As shown in the FT-IR spectra, the OH groups in cotton could be determined from the stretching vibration band at 2999 to $3666\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ [84, 85]. The peaks at 2905 and $2844\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ were attributed to C-H stretching vibrations [86].
The peak at $1648~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ was from the bending vibration of absorbed water [87]. The peaks at 1372 and $1314~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ were assigned to OH and C-H bending vibrations, the peaks at 1163 and $1106\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ were assigned to C-O and C-C stretching vibrations, these peaks confirmed the C-O-C asymmetric stretching vibration in the glycosidic ring [88]. The peak at around $1054\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ was due to C-O symmetric stretching. The peaks at 897 and $663~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ were attributed to C-H out of bending vibration and OH out of plane bending, respectively [89]. These FT-IR analysis indicated that the introduction of PCN-224 into cotton fabrics did not change the structure of cotton cellulose. However, new peaks of 1600 and $1415~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ could be noted in PCN-224/WCF, which were attributed to $\mathrm{C=C}$ stretching vibration and C-N bending vibration from PCN-224 [90]. In terms of all FT-IR analysis, PCN-224 was successfully introduced into cotton fabrics without affecting its chemical structure.
XRD was further used to confirm that PCN-224 nanoparticles were deposited on cotton fiber surface. Figure 2 panel D shows the XRD patterns, diffraction peaks were at around $2\Theta{=}14.9$ ° [1-10], $16.5~^{\circ}$ [110], and $22.7\mathrm{~}^{\circ}\mathrm{~}[200]$ , which meant unchanged cellulose I crystalline structure of PCN-224/WCF. While some new diffraction peaks at around 4.70 °, 6.58 °, 7.98 °, 9.30 °, and $10.46^{\circ}$ could be observed (Figure S2), indicating successful deposition of PCN-224 nanoparticles.
To further confirm the successful introduction of PCN-224 into WCF, EDS was employed to observe the elemental distribution. From Figure 3 panel C-G, as expected, carbon $(45.06~\mathrm{wt\%})$ ), nitrogen $(3.41~\mathrm{wt\%})$ ), oxygen $(47.00~\mathrm{wt\%})$ , and zirconium (4.53 $w t\%$ ) were uniformly distributed along the fiber. An obvious signal at around $2.1\mathrm{keV}$ in Figure 3 panel A was due to Zr element, indicating PCN-224 was successfully introduced.
## 3.2 Physical properties of WCF and PCN-224/WCF
Physical properties including tensile strength and thermostability were known to be related to practical applications of textiles. In order to evaluate how PCN-224 nanoparticles influence the mechanical properties of the materials, the tensile strength of WCF, PCN-224/WCF, and photoaged PCN-224/WCF were conducted. As was shown in the Figure 4 panel A, WCF processed the best mechanical breaking elongation $(22.82\%)$ with breaking strength at 9.21 MPa. Decreased breaking elongation $(15.42\%)$ and breaking strength $(3.63~\mathrm{MPa}),$ ) could be observed for PCN224/WCF, the degraded performance might be due to the corrosion of the acidic environment during the PCN-224 synthesis process. After illumination, the photoaged fabrics showed the worst breaking elongation $(8.76\%)$ and breaking strength (3.17 MPa). The test results of mechanical properties are consistent with those described in the literatures, the reason may be due to the following: (i) cracking and thermal expansion of fibers leading to the decrease of mechanical property [91]; (ii) changes of the fibrous structure and fibrous physicochemical properties from the photooxidation [92].
TGA and DTG curves of WCF and PCN-224/WCF were shown in Figure 4 panel B and C. A slight weight loss of about $5\%$ below $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for both samples was due to the absorbed moisture. As the temperature goes up, the initial pyrolysis temperatures were ${\sim}194~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for WCF/PCN-224 and ${\sim}216~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for WCF, and the decomposition temperature range was about 194-491 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ mainly due to the organic decomposition. When the temperature reached $600^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and the weight remained stable, the weight losses of WCF and PCN-224/WCF were $86.83\%$ and $83.46\%$ , respectively. From the TGA analysis, the increased proportion is about $3.37\%$ which is different from the result from weighting method above. We ascribe this from the simultaneous thermal decomposition of cotton fiber and PCN-224 nanoparticles. From Figure 4 panel C, the absorption peaks centered at around $374^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $346^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ revealed that the introduction of PCN-224 did not significantly affect the thermostability of WCF. Overall, the TGA and DTG data suggested that PCN-224 nanoparticles growth onto cotton fiber did not affect the thermal behavior, the thermal stability of the materials is sufficient for practical applications.
3.3 Antibacterial activity and MTT assay.
In vitro aPDI studies with PCN-224/WCF were performed against Gram-negative E. coli and $P$ . aeruginosa as well as Gram-positive S. aureus and B. subtilis. The bacterial survival rate is shown in Figure 5, all the studies were carried out in triplicate with 1 h dark incubation and $45\mathrm{min}$ illumination. Fixed illumination condition: a 500 W Xe lamp, $\lambda{\ge}420~\mathrm{nm}$ , $45~\mathrm{min}$ , $15\ \mathrm{cm}$ . The original concentration of PBS bacterial solution was $10^{8}\mathrm{CFU/mL}$ which was determined by colony counting.
From all the bacterial inactivation study, the dark control group (S. aureus) exhibited $0.775\%$ inactivation (0.0034 log unit) and the WCF control group (S. aureus) exhibited $3.876\%$ inactivation (0.0172 log unit), the inactivation values of other kinds of bacterial inactivation control groups were lower than that of $S.$ . aureus, all these demonstrated the necessity for PSs and light illumination. The dark control inactivation results were different from previous study which used porphyrin-conjugated regenerated cellulose nanofibers (termed RC-TETA-PPIX-Zn) as anti-infective nanomaterials [93]. RC-TETA-PPIX-Zn exhibited $96\%$ inactivation against $S.$ aureus with 40 min dark incubation, the reason was RC-TETA-PPIX-Zn had a high loading of 412 nmol PPIX/mg, making it extraordinarily sensitive to bacteria even in the darkest condition that can be achieved in a laboratory. While PCN-224/WCF showed no bacterial activation in dark, we surmised that the reason may be due to the following:
(i) light scattering caused by the thick cotton fibers weakened the intensity of the light, the minimal ambient room light was not strong enough to motivate photodynamism; (ii) the fiber interlacing structure sequestered some PS, reducing the ability of PS to function.
Meanwhile for the photodynamic inactivation groups, as expected, the data showed that all kinds of bacterial strains were susceptible to photodynamic inactivation. After 1 h dark preincubation and $45~\mathrm{{min}}$ illumination, the PCN-224/WCF exhibited a high efficient photobactericidal effect, four kinds of bacterial strains were eliminated by $99.9999\%$ (E. coli: 6 log units, $P{<}0.0001$ ) (S. aureus: 6 log units, $P{<}0.0001$ ) ( $B$ . subtilis: 6 log units, $P{<}0.0005$ ) ( $P.$ . aeruginosa: 6 log units, $P{<}0.0001\$ ). That is to say, no CFU could be observed after illumination. It was well accepted that Gram-negative bacteria strains are more resistant to photodynamic inactivation because they have an extra layer of lipopolysaccharide (LPS) which is highly impermeable [94], but our results showed no difference in antibacterial effect, indicating the broad spectrum antibacterial property of PCN-224/WCF. Overall, these bacterial inactivation results demonstrated that PCN-224/WCF could achieve the detection limit inactivation of $E$ . coli, S. aureus, B. subtilis, and $P.$ . aeruginosa, and showed equal or better level of antibacterial inactivation than some other PS-loaded materials, such as methylene bluedyed polyester (S. aureus: $99.89\%$ ) [95], methylene blue decorated nanofiber ( $E.$ . coli: $97\%$ , S. aureus: $99.997\%$ ) [96], polysulfopropylbetaine finished cotton (E. coli: $99.69\%$ , S. aureus: $99.95\%$ ) [97], rose Bengal embedded blended fabrics (E. coli: 1-2 log units reduction, $P.$ . aeruginosa: 1-2 log units reduction, S. aureus: $99.98\%$ , B. subtilis:
$99.993\%$ ) [12], and RC-TETA-PPIX-Zn (E. coli: $99.999\%$ , S. aureus: $99.999\%$ ) [93].
E. coli was chosen to explore the antibacterial property of PCN-224/WCF as a function of illumination time (Figure S4 panel A). After 1 h dark preincubation and 15 min illumination, the PCN-224/WCF exhibited no inactivation towards $E$ coli, however, a $96.987\%$ elimination in illumination time to 30 min could be observed (1.52 log units, $P{<}0.0001$ ). As talked before, the PCN-224/WCF inactivated E. coli to the detect limitation of 6 log units with $45\ \mathrm{min}$ illumination. The antibacterial property against $E$ . coli affected by washing times was further explored (Figure S4 panel B). After once and twice washing, PCN-224/WCF still exhibited antibacterial property towards $E$ . coli under $45\ \mathrm{min}$ illumination $98.489\%$ inactivation, 1.82 log units, $P{<}0.0001$ ; $67.556\%$ inactivation, 0.49 log units, $P{<}0.0001\rangle$ .
L929 cells were employed to evaluate the in vitro cytotoxicity and biocompatibility of PCN-224/WCF. Figure 5 panel E and F demonstrate the experimental results. As shown in Figure 5 panel F, after co-cultured with PCN224/WCF for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , L929 cells still displayed fusiform shape, which meant cells lived in a healthy state. From the insert in Figure 5 panel F, the relative survival rate was higher than $80\%$ , suggesting PCN-224/WCF had good biocompatibility, which can be used as human friendly self-disinfecting textile materials for antibacterial inactivation and infection prevention.
## 3.4 Detection of ROS $(^{1}\mathbf{O}_{2})$
Figure 6 panel A showed the ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ generation process of PS molecules. Briefly, PS molecules absorb light forming excited singlet state, then the excited singlet state PSs emit fluorescence. Besides, intersystem crossing happens to generate the excited triplet state of PSs. Finally, ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ could be generated through energy transfer from the excited triplet state of the PSs to the ground state ${}^{3}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . From the literature, DPBF is widely used as a colorimetric trapping agent for ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ detection. DPBF could react with ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ with high specificity which could be bleached to colorless 1,2-dibenzoylbenzene [98]. Under the illumination of a $532\ \mathrm{nm}$ laser in the presence of PCN-224/WCF, the $410\ \mathrm{nm}$ absorbance of DPBF gradually decreased (Figure 6 panel B). However, when the bleaching studies were carried out in absolute methanol (WCF-present illumination control, Figure 6 panel C) or without laser (PCN-224/WCF-present dark control, Figure 6 panel D), no obvious decrease of DPBF absorbance spectrum could be observed. Taken together, the DPBF bleaching study results provided convincing evidence for the formation of ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ under visible light from PCN-224/WCF.
## 4. CONCLUSIONS
According to the reported MOFs coating approaches [10, 15, 71, 72], this work provided an ease-fabricated and effectively light-driven antibacterial textile materials to reduce the possibility of microbial infection and transmission. The PCN-224 nanoparticles with an average of $88~\mathrm{nm}$ were chosen as the antibacterial agent, which were distributed uniformly on the cotton fiber surface by in situ growth method. The main advantages of PCN-224/WCF were reflected in the facile preparative approaches and efficient sterilization performance. The characterizations of the material proved that PCN-224 was successfully deposited on the cotton fiber, and the appearance color change from white to brown-red may simplify the subsequent dyeing process [78]. The physical differences from WCF and PCN-224/WCF revealed PCN-224 growth affected tensile property greatly, but had little effect on thermal behavior. The photobactericidal property revealed that although Gram-negative bacteria are known to be resistant to photodynamic inactivation. PCN-224/WCF showed no difference in inactivation between Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria $(99.9999\%$ inactivation in $45\mathrm{min}\$ ) though Gram-negative bacteria are known to be resistant to photodynamic inactivation. Unlike previous study [93], PCN-224/WCF exhibited no inactivation towards bacteria in dark, and the reason might be that: (i) the light scattering caused by the cotton fibers reduced the intensity of the light; (ii) the special structure of the woven fabric prevented part of the PCN-224 nanoparticles to function. Compared to other PS-loaded materials in this field [12, 93, 95-97], our material showed equal or better level of antibacterial inactivation. Although the material showed strong antibacterial effect, it had low cytotoxicity and good biocompatibility. Taken together, these findings provided a facile strategy to employ aPDI in textile materials, but further study on increasing ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ generation is under way to provide more efficient self-disinfecting textiles for infection prevention.
